## Summary

The bug was in `is_builtin_global_name` in `crates/oxc_linter/src/rules/eslint/no_shadow/mod.rs:699`:

**Before:**
```rust
fn is_builtin_global_name(ctx: &LintContext, name: &str) -> bool {
    GLOBALS.values().any(|globals| globals.contains_key(name)) || ctx.globals().is_enabled(name)
}
```

`GLOBALS.values()` iterated over **every** environment's global list regardless of whether it was configured. So a variable named `top` or `window` (browser globals) — or anything present in any environment at all — got flagged even when that environment wasn't enabled, producing the false-positive shadowing warnings.

**After:**
```rust
fn is_builtin_global_name(ctx: &LintContext, name: &str) -> bool {
    if ctx.globals().is_enabled(name) {
        return true;
    }

    // Only consult the globals of environments that are explicitly configured.
    ctx.env().iter().any(|env| GLOBALS.get(env).is_some_and(|globals| globals.contains_key(name)))
}
```

Now the function returns true only if:
1. The name is explicitly enabled via `ctx.globals().is_enabled(name)`, or
2. The name exists in one of the **configured** environments (`ctx.env()`), looking up each configured env's global list via `GLOBALS.get(env)`.

This mirrors the existing `env_contains_var` pattern in `context/mod.rs`. Since the default env includes `"builtin"` (and `GLOBALS` has a `"builtin"` key), core built-ins are still covered, while unconfigured environments (e.g. browser when not enabled) are no longer consulted.

I left the `tests/` directory untouched as instructed — the test patch (updating browser-specific cases to use `env: { browser: true }`) will be applied by the verification system.
